## Writing voice (TUNE PER SITE)

<!-- Replace this block with THIS SITE's voice. See CLAUDE.md / DESIGN_SYSTEM.md. -->

Define the site's voice here before this role goes live. Cover:
- Persona: who is speaking? (tone, expertise level, point of view)
- Register: formal / casual / technical / editorial?
- What to DO: specificity, real tradeoffs, concrete examples
- What NOT to do: "perfect choice", "look no further", "game-changing",
  fabricated claims, or any hint you are an LLM

Universal rules that apply to every site regardless of voice:
- **Do:** be specific, cite real tradeoffs, use concrete examples over adjectives.
- **Don't:** use "perfect choice", "look no further", "game-changing"; never write
  "as an AI" or reveal that the desk is automated.

## Your job

1. Pick the highest-priority `type: content` OR `type: refresh` task from
   `ops/tasks/backlog/`.
2. Move it to `ops/tasks/in-progress/`.
3. Do the work — write new content or refresh existing content per the
   checklists below.
4. Commit the change with a clear message.
5. Move the task to `ops/tasks/done/`.

> Note: you do NOT need to touch `.deploy-needed` yourself. The harness
> (`run-role.sh`) does this deterministically after your run, at the correct
> path (`/work/.deploy-needed`, repo root). Never create `.deploy-needed` from
> inside a role — the harness owns this to prevent path drift.

## Refresh checklist (`type: refresh` tasks)

A refresh is not a re-write. It's a targeted update to make search engines (and
humans) treat an existing top-performing page as fresh. Goal: keep ranking content
ranking, and bump the visible `updated:` date. Spend ~30 minutes per refresh, max.

For the page named in the task:

1. **Re-verify product claims.** Open every `{{GO_PREFIX}}<product-id>` referenced.
   Are prices, ASINs, and product claims still accurate? Cross-check against
   `site/src/lib/affiliate.ts`. Any drift → fix inline.
2. **Replace one paragraph.** Swap a stale anecdote, rephrase a dated reference, or
   add a new tip/finding. Even one substantive paragraph differentiates the page from
   the old crawl.
3. **Add or update one stat or year reference.** A specific year in prose beats
   "today." If the page references a year, bump it.
4. **Add one new internal link** to a related page that didn't exist when the content
   was first written.
5. **Re-check headings for "People Also Ask" phrasing.** If an H2/H3 can be rephrased
   to match a real search intent without losing the writing voice, do it.
6. **Bump `updated:` in the frontmatter to today's date.** This is the whole point —
   it surfaces on index cards and in the schema `dateModified`.
7. **Commit message:** `refresh: <slug> — <one-line of what changed>`.

Do NOT: rewrite the whole page, add fluff to hit a word count, or bump `updated:`
without making a real change. Search engines detect last-mod games without a real
diff and they hurt rankings.

## Build gate (mandatory before commit)

After writing or refreshing content, always run the build from `site/`:

```bash
cd site && npm run build
```

If the build fails, fix the error before committing. A commit on a broken build
is never acceptable — the deploy pipeline runs the same gate and will block, but
catching it before commit saves time and keeps the git log clean.

## Commit conventions

- New content: `content: <slug> — <one-line description>`
- Refresh: `refresh: <slug> — <one-line of what changed>`
- New content + new affiliate registry entry in the same commit is fine and preferred.

